1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:11,483
In April 1928, twelve jurors at the Old Bailey were handed a microscope, and asked to look at the bottom of one brass cartridge.

2
00:00:11,932 --> 00:00:18,374
It was an army cartridge, fired and empty, the kind made at Woolwich before the First World War.

3
00:00:18,374 --> 00:00:24,816
On its copper cap, a gunmaker said, there were two faint marks, side by side, at twelve o'clock.

4
00:00:24,816 --> 00:00:28,036
They were not on any other cartridge in England.

5
00:00:28,036 --> 00:00:31,526
Only on the ones fired from one particular revolver.

6
00:00:31,975 --> 00:00:40,078
Seven months earlier, on a dark road in Essex, a village policeman had been shot four times beside a stolen car.

7
00:00:40,078 --> 00:00:43,333
Two of those shots had gone through his eyes.

8
00:00:43,783 --> 00:00:46,852
Ever since, one story has followed this case.

9
00:00:46,852 --> 00:00:56,263
The killer, it is said, believed that a dead man's eyes kept the last thing they saw, like a photograph, and fired to destroy the picture.

10
00:00:56,263 --> 00:00:57,559
It is a good story.

11
00:00:57,559 --> 00:00:59,877
The trial record does not tell it.

12
00:01:00,328 --> 00:01:12,946
What the record does tell is the story of a revolver, a dropped cartridge, and a flaw on a piece of steel so small that it had to be photographed and enlarged before a jury could see it.

13
00:01:12,946 --> 00:01:16,677
A flaw that would decide whether two men lived or died.

14
00:01:17,128 --> 00:01:18,976
This is Precinct Archive.

15
00:01:18,976 --> 00:01:23,707
And this is the case that taught Britain the phrase a gun print.

16
00:01:25,557 --> 00:01:38,004
Stapleford Abbotts, in 1927, is a scattering of farms and cottages in the Essex countryside, between Romford and Ongar, about twenty miles from the centre of London.

17
00:01:38,004 --> 00:01:46,302
Its policeman is George William Gutteridge, Police Constable four eight nine of the Essex County Constabulary.

18
00:01:46,751 --> 00:01:48,077
He is thirty-six.

19
00:01:48,077 --> 00:02:01,955
He was born at Downham Market in Norfolk, joined the Essex police in 1910, and served in the Machine Gun Corps near the end of the First World War before going back to the force.

20
00:02:01,955 --> 00:02:05,853
Since 1922 he has been the village constable here.

21
00:02:06,304 --> 00:02:12,953
He lives at Towneley Cottages with his wife, Rose, and their two children, aged twelve and four.

22
00:02:12,953 --> 00:02:16,001
A village constable is never quite off duty.

23
00:02:16,001 --> 00:02:20,435
He works days, he works nights, and much of his work is walking.

24
00:02:20,884 --> 00:02:26,374
On Monday, the 26th of September, he comes home from the day shift at about six.

25
00:02:26,374 --> 00:02:28,570
He has his meal with his family.

26
00:02:28,570 --> 00:02:36,531
At eleven that night, he goes out again, in uniform, with his heavy police cape over his shoulders against the cold.

27
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:39,485
Night duty in rural Essex has a ritual.

28
00:02:39,485 --> 00:02:46,483
Constables from neighbouring beats meet at fixed points at fixed times, so that each knows the other is safe.

29
00:02:46,483 --> 00:02:52,519
At about three in the morning, Gutteridge meets PC Sydney Taylor at a place called Howe Green.

30
00:02:52,519 --> 00:02:53,931
They talk for a while.

31
00:02:53,931 --> 00:02:59,645
At about twenty-five past three, they part, and Gutteridge sets off on foot towards home.

32
00:03:00,096 --> 00:03:07,003
On the same night, fifteen miles away in Billericay, a doctor's car is stolen from his garage.

33
00:03:07,003 --> 00:03:11,411
It is a blue Morris Cowley, registration T W six one two oh.

34
00:03:11,411 --> 00:03:16,041
The doctor, Edward Lovell, keeps his medical instruments in it.

35
00:03:16,491 --> 00:03:18,008
Hold on to that number.

36
00:03:18,008 --> 00:03:27,379
Within a few minutes of Gutteridge leaving Howe Green, that car is coming along the lane towards him, and he steps out and signals it to stop.

37
00:03:27,829 --> 00:03:32,380
Here, the case file has a problem that matters to everything that follows.

38
00:03:32,380 --> 00:03:36,562
Only two people saw what happened next, apart from the man who died.

39
00:03:36,562 --> 00:03:39,207
One of them never told the police anything.

40
00:03:39,207 --> 00:03:44,619
The other gave his account only after he was arrested, and his account blamed the first.

41
00:03:44,619 --> 00:03:51,261
Everything in the next few minutes comes from that one statement, made by a man trying to save his own life.

42
00:03:51,711 --> 00:03:58,963
According to that statement, the constable asked where they had come from, and asked the driver for his licence.

43
00:03:58,963 --> 00:04:00,258
The driver had none.

44
00:04:00,258 --> 00:04:02,524
The passenger said the car was his.

45
00:04:02,524 --> 00:04:07,963
The constable shone his torch into both their faces and asked the number of the car.

46
00:04:07,963 --> 00:04:09,388
The passenger gave it.

47
00:04:09,388 --> 00:04:11,330
Very well, the constable said.

48
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:12,754
I'll take particulars.

49
00:04:13,205 --> 00:04:15,723
He took out his notebook and a pencil.

50
00:04:15,723 --> 00:04:21,491
And while he was writing, the driver shot him twice, through the left side of his face.

51
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:27,704
The statement says the constable fell back against the bank, still alive, his eyes open.

52
00:04:27,704 --> 00:04:33,337
The driver got out, stood over him and said, What are you looking at me like that for?

53
00:04:33,337 --> 00:04:38,183
Then he stooped, and fired twice more, at close range, once into each eye.

54
00:04:38,634 --> 00:04:41,832
The car drove on, through fog, towards London.

55
00:04:41,832 --> 00:04:45,932
The passenger was told to reload the revolver as they went.

56
00:04:45,932 --> 00:04:53,299
And in his hurry, by his own account, he dropped one of the empty cartridge cases on the floor of the car.

57
00:04:53,749 --> 00:05:05,361
At about six in the morning, a postman named William Ward drives along the road in the half-light and sees a man sitting against the bank, with his legs out across the carriageway.

58
00:05:05,361 --> 00:05:06,909
It is George Gutteridge.

59
00:05:06,909 --> 00:05:07,619
He is dead.

60
00:05:07,619 --> 00:05:10,393
His helmet and his notebook are beside him.

61
00:05:10,393 --> 00:05:13,619
In his right hand, he is still holding the pencil.

62
00:05:14,069 --> 00:05:15,519
Help is slow to come.

63
00:05:15,519 --> 00:05:24,016
In the country in 1927, the nearest telephone might be a public call box, or a post office, or a police station miles away.

64
00:05:24,016 --> 00:05:33,549
According to one local history, a passing bus driver tried to ring from a call box and the operator would not connect him without payment.

65
00:05:33,549 --> 00:05:37,971
The postman went back to his post office and telephoned Romford.

66
00:05:39,821 --> 00:05:47,243
That same morning, the stolen Morris Cowley is found abandoned in a quiet side street in Brixton, south London.

67
00:05:47,243 --> 00:05:49,650
There is blood on the running board.

68
00:05:49,650 --> 00:05:57,408
Because the car has turned up in London, Scotland Yard is called in, and Chief Inspector James Berrett takes charge.

69
00:05:57,858 --> 00:06:09,923
Under a seat in the car, the police find what the passenger dropped: an empty brass cartridge case, point four five five calibre, the size used in the army's Webley revolvers.

70
00:06:09,923 --> 00:06:14,541
Stamped on its base are the letters R L and the Roman numeral four.

71
00:06:14,541 --> 00:06:16,954
Royal Laboratory, Woolwich Arsenal.

72
00:06:16,954 --> 00:06:17,644
Mark Four.

73
00:06:18,093 --> 00:06:20,042
That stamp narrows the field.

74
00:06:20,042 --> 00:06:26,562
Mark Four cartridges had been withdrawn from the army around the outbreak of the First World War.

75
00:06:26,562 --> 00:06:32,075
They were old stock, kept in drawers and kitbags by men who had brought them home.

76
00:06:32,524 --> 00:06:34,406
The wounds narrow it further.

77
00:06:34,406 --> 00:06:44,465
Doctors and gun experts could see that at least one of the shots had been fired with black powder, which leaves heavy marks of burnt grains around a wound.

78
00:06:44,465 --> 00:06:50,305
Black powder had not been used in service revolver ammunition since the eighteen nineties.

79
00:06:50,305 --> 00:06:54,848
Whoever fired that gun was loading it with a jumble of old cartridges.

80
00:06:55,298 --> 00:06:59,097
So the police know what kind of gun they are looking for.

81
00:06:59,097 --> 00:07:06,760
A Webley point four five five service revolver, of which there were hundreds of thousands in Britain after the war.

82
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:09,159
What they need is not a kind of gun.

83
00:07:09,159 --> 00:07:10,091
It is one gun.

84
00:07:10,540 --> 00:07:17,256
On the 30th of September, the inquest returns a verdict of murder by person or persons unknown.

85
00:07:17,256 --> 00:07:25,598
Through the autumn, revolver after revolver that comes into police hands is tested against the cartridge from the car.

86
00:07:25,598 --> 00:07:27,083
None of them matches.

87
00:07:27,533 --> 00:07:33,207
This is where the legend of the eyes begins to grow, and it is worth stopping to weigh it.

88
00:07:33,207 --> 00:07:43,924
In the eighteen seventies, a German scientist, Wilhelm Kooner, had shown that a rabbit's retina, treated quickly after death, could hold a faint image of a bright window.

89
00:07:43,924 --> 00:07:50,923
Newspapers turned that into a popular belief: that a murdered person's eyes might keep a picture of the killer.

90
00:07:51,372 --> 00:07:55,906
Writers on this case have often said the eyes were shot for that reason.

91
00:07:55,906 --> 00:08:01,133
But no witness at the trial said so, and no document in the trial volume claims it.

92
00:08:01,133 --> 00:08:08,941
The only account of the moment gives a different reason in the killer's own words: what are you looking at me like that for.

93
00:08:08,941 --> 00:08:13,034
That is a man reacting to a stare, not a man destroying evidence.

94
00:08:13,034 --> 00:08:15,741
The superstition may have been in his head.

95
00:08:15,741 --> 00:08:19,645
That is an inference, and the file cannot prove it either way.

96
00:08:19,645 --> 00:08:21,975
What the file does show is the irony.

97
00:08:21,975 --> 00:08:28,964
The killer worried about the dead man's eyes, and was caught by the cartridge his partner dropped on the floor.

98
00:08:29,415 --> 00:08:30,118
Weeks pass.

99
00:08:30,118 --> 00:08:42,079
Then, according to the memoirs of Percy Sillitoe, chief constable of Sheffield at the time, a minor car accident in Sheffield in November brought in a local criminal named Charles Currie.

100
00:08:42,079 --> 00:08:48,412
Currie, in Sillitoe's telling, named the driver of that car, and linked him to the murder in Essex.

101
00:08:48,412 --> 00:08:52,889
Sillitoe says he was on the telephone to Scotland Yard within seconds.

102
00:08:53,341 --> 00:08:55,627
The name was Frederick Guy Browne.

103
00:08:55,627 --> 00:09:01,411
He ran a small motor garage in Northcote Road, near Clapham Junction, in south London.

104
00:09:01,411 --> 00:09:06,725
He was in his forties, very tall, a skilled mechanic, and a professional thief.

105
00:09:06,725 --> 00:09:13,787
He had served sentences in Parkhurst and Dartmoor, and he had been out of Dartmoor only since the spring.

106
00:09:14,236 --> 00:09:19,804
His speciality was stealing cars and changing them enough that they could be sold.

107
00:09:19,804 --> 00:09:29,989
Working for him in the garage, as a handyman who slept in a back room, was a Scotsman with a string of aliases and convictions, William Henry Kennedy.

108
00:09:30,438 --> 00:09:34,120
The police wait, and watch, and build a theft case first.

109
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:40,063
On the 20th of January, 1928, officers raid the garage and arrest Browne for stealing a car.

110
00:09:40,063 --> 00:09:43,874
In the car he is using, they find a loaded Webley revolver.

111
00:09:43,874 --> 00:09:48,848
Behind the driver's seat, in a hidden compartment, they find a second Webley.

112
00:09:48,848 --> 00:09:56,794
In the garage and at his home, they find more guns and boxes of old ammunition, including cartridges stamped R L Mark Four.

113
00:09:57,245 --> 00:10:00,147
They also find a set of surgical instruments.

114
00:10:00,147 --> 00:10:04,211
Doctor Lovell's bag of instruments had been taken with his car.

115
00:10:04,211 --> 00:10:08,727
Browne said he had bought such things to use as tools in his workshop.

116
00:10:08,727 --> 00:10:14,662
Hold on to those instruments, because the owner could not swear in court that they were his.

117
00:10:15,110 --> 00:10:17,035
Kennedy is not at the garage.

118
00:10:17,035 --> 00:10:26,128
He has seen the police there, and gone north to Liverpool, where he takes a room in Copperas Hill, a short walk from Lime Street station.

119
00:10:26,128 --> 00:10:29,711
On the 25th of January, detectives come for him there.

120
00:10:30,162 --> 00:10:39,649
As Detective Sergeant William Mattinson closes in, Kennedy pulls an automatic pistol, presses it towards him and shouts, stand back, or I'll shoot you.

121
00:10:39,649 --> 00:10:40,968
He pulls the trigger.

122
00:10:40,968 --> 00:10:41,973
Nothing happens.

123
00:10:41,973 --> 00:10:43,418
The safety catch is on.

124
00:10:43,418 --> 00:10:47,691
Later, in the police station, Kennedy tells the sergeant, I'm sorry.

125
00:10:47,691 --> 00:10:53,722
I've no grudge against the police, but you should be in Heaven by now, and there was one for me.

126
00:10:54,173 --> 00:11:04,166
So by the end of January 1928, Scotland Yard has two men, two guns, a box of old army cartridges, and one empty case from a stolen car.

127
00:11:04,166 --> 00:11:08,311
Browne says he was at home with his wife all that night.

128
00:11:08,311 --> 00:11:14,086
Could a piece of brass prove a man was on that lane, when he swore he was not?

129
00:11:15,934 --> 00:11:20,353
Kennedy is brought to London by train and held at New Scotland Yard.

130
00:11:20,353 --> 00:11:22,823
His wife of one week comes to see him.

131
00:11:22,823 --> 00:11:29,451
According to the police account read at the trial, she asked him, why, you didn't murder him, did you?

132
00:11:29,451 --> 00:11:31,140
No, I didn't, he answered.

133
00:11:31,140 --> 00:11:33,480
But I was there, and I know who did.

134
00:11:33,929 --> 00:11:36,747
That night Kennedy makes a long statement.

135
00:11:36,747 --> 00:11:43,925
It is the account of the lane told earlier in this video: the stop, the questions, the notebook, the shots.

136
00:11:43,925 --> 00:11:48,553
In it, Kennedy is the frightened passenger, and Browne is the gunman.

137
00:11:48,553 --> 00:11:51,303
Browne, faced with it, denies everything.

138
00:11:51,754 --> 00:11:58,229
Here is the part of the case that is easy to miss, and it is the reason the gun mattered so much.

139
00:11:58,229 --> 00:12:04,771
In English law, a statement by one accused person is evidence only against the person who made it.

140
00:12:04,771 --> 00:12:08,710
Both the magistrate and the trial judge ruled exactly that.

141
00:12:08,710 --> 00:12:11,046
Kennedy's story could hang Kennedy.

142
00:12:11,046 --> 00:12:14,184
In law, it could not be used to convict Browne.

143
00:12:14,634 --> 00:12:18,365
Strip the statement away, and what is left against Browne?

144
00:12:18,365 --> 00:12:20,359
No witness saw him on the lane.

145
00:12:20,359 --> 00:12:23,318
The doctor could not identify his instruments.

146
00:12:23,318 --> 00:12:25,505
Browne had an alibi from his wife.

147
00:12:25,505 --> 00:12:29,558
What was left was ammunition of the right kind, and a revolver.

148
00:12:29,558 --> 00:12:37,021
If the prosecution could not tie that revolver to that cartridge, the case against Browne would be very thin indeed.

149
00:12:37,471 --> 00:12:48,670
The trial opens at the Central Criminal Court on Monday, the 23rd of April, 1928, before Mister Justice Avory, a judge with a reputation for severity.

150
00:12:48,670 --> 00:12:53,299
The Solicitor-General, Sir Boyd Merriman, leads for the Crown.

151
00:12:53,299 --> 00:12:56,061
Each prisoner has his own barristers.

152
00:12:56,061 --> 00:12:59,944
Their application to be tried separately is refused.

153
00:13:00,394 --> 00:13:03,944
Then the gun experts are called, one after another.

154
00:13:03,944 --> 00:13:16,544
The first, from Woolwich Arsenal, produces a packet of twelve Mark Four cartridges unopened since it was packed in 1913, and says the case from the car was stamped by the same tool.

155
00:13:16,544 --> 00:13:20,860
Then comes Robert Churchill, a gunmaker from Leicester Square.

156
00:13:21,311 --> 00:13:24,651
Churchill explains what happens when a revolver fires.

157
00:13:24,651 --> 00:13:34,920
The explosion drives the cartridge backwards against a steel plate at the back of the gun, the breech shield, so hard that the soft copper cap takes an imprint of it.

158
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:44,509
Under the microscope, he tells the court, he can see that this cartridge was fired from this Webley revolver, and could not have been fired from any other.

159
00:13:44,958 --> 00:13:55,736
He has also fired both of Browne's revolvers into a pigskin target, at three, six and twelve inches, and compared the scorch marks with photographs of the wounds.

160
00:13:55,736 --> 00:13:59,727
The two shots to the cheek, he says, were smokeless cordite.

161
00:13:59,727 --> 00:14:02,787
The shot under the right eye was black powder.

162
00:14:02,787 --> 00:14:04,850
One gun, loaded with a mixture.

163
00:14:05,301 --> 00:14:14,476
Then William Fox, chief examiner of small arms at the Royal Small Arms Factory at Enfield Lock, with twenty-seven years' experience.

164
00:14:14,476 --> 00:14:17,466
Asked for his opinion, he refuses the word.

165
00:14:17,466 --> 00:14:19,829
I do not give an opinion, he says.

166
00:14:19,829 --> 00:14:22,957
I say that case was fired from that revolver.

167
00:14:22,957 --> 00:14:25,807
The judge asks if he means without doubt.

168
00:14:25,807 --> 00:14:27,198
Absolutely, my lord.

169
00:14:27,647 --> 00:14:36,139
Fox points to two small dents in the steel of the breech shield, just above the hole for the firing pin, at the twelve o'clock position.

170
00:14:36,139 --> 00:14:41,759
They were made, he says, by a succession of blows, so their edges are rough and irregular.

171
00:14:41,759 --> 00:14:48,754
And on the cap of the cartridge from the car, in the same position, the same shapes are pressed into the copper.

172
00:14:49,205 --> 00:14:56,970
And then, the trial record notes, the jury were handed a microscope, so that they could examine the cartridge for themselves.

173
00:14:56,970 --> 00:15:01,940
A Woolwich chemist, George Perry, showed photographic enlargements of the marks.

174
00:15:01,940 --> 00:15:10,824
Fox told the court he had searched the whole stock of service revolvers waiting for repair at Enfield, and had found none marked like this one.

175
00:15:11,274 --> 00:15:14,525
How strong was this evidence, by modern standards?

176
00:15:14,525 --> 00:15:24,021
It rested on the judgement of experienced men, not on a database or a statistic, and Fox's word, absolutely, would make a modern scientist uneasy.

177
00:15:24,021 --> 00:15:32,476
But the logic is the one firearms examiners still use: tool marks made by hand-finishing are unique enough to point to one weapon.

178
00:15:32,476 --> 00:15:35,402
The editor of the trial volume put it simply.

179
00:15:35,402 --> 00:15:37,223
Why not call it a gun print?

180
00:15:37,673 --> 00:15:41,894
Browne gives evidence himself, and argues with almost everyone.

181
00:15:41,894 --> 00:15:48,659
At first he refuses the oath, saying he does not know the whole truth and so cannot swear to tell it.

182
00:15:48,659 --> 00:15:55,224
He explains the guns as protection, the stolen goods as other men's, and the instruments as tools.

183
00:15:55,224 --> 00:15:58,440
Kennedy does not go into the witness box at all.

184
00:15:58,889 --> 00:16:05,724
On the 27th of April, the jury retire at eight minutes to one, taking the exhibits with them.

185
00:16:05,724 --> 00:16:07,929
They return at ten past three.

186
00:16:07,929 --> 00:16:12,338
Both men, guilty of the murder of George William Gutteridge.

187
00:16:12,788 --> 00:16:25,153
Asked if he has anything to say, Browne thanks the judge and his counsel, and tells the court that the jury have had stuff given to them that is not genuine, and that his conscience is clear.

188
00:16:25,153 --> 00:16:28,972
Kennedy says it was fate, and that he is not afraid to die.

189
00:16:28,972 --> 00:16:30,525
He asks to see his wife.

190
00:16:30,975 --> 00:16:35,636
Their appeal, on the refusal of separate trials, is dismissed.

191
00:16:35,636 --> 00:16:50,824
At nine in the morning on Thursday, the 31st of May, 1928, Frederick Browne is hanged at Pentonville, and William Kennedy at Wandsworth, at the same hour, in two prisons on opposite sides of the Thames.

192
00:16:52,673 --> 00:16:54,790
Doubts have been raised since.

193
00:16:54,790 --> 00:17:05,796
One person close to the trial wrote that he did not think Browne was the kind of man to fire the shots into the eyes, and others have suggested Kennedy did.

194
00:17:05,796 --> 00:17:08,124
Those are opinions, not evidence.

195
00:17:08,124 --> 00:17:17,084
In 2020, the case was examined again for a BBC series, and a retired judge, David Radford, concluded that the verdict was safe.

196
00:17:17,084 --> 00:17:27,173
Whoever's hand held the gun on the lane, the gun was Browne's, the car was stolen by both of them, and the jury found they were in it together.

197
00:17:27,623 --> 00:17:35,368
What changed after this case was not the science, which had been developing for years in Britain and America, but its fame.

198
00:17:35,368 --> 00:17:39,272
It became known as the case of the men hanged by a microscope.

199
00:17:39,272 --> 00:17:45,568
Robert Churchill became the expert the police turned to in firearms cases for the next twenty years.

200
00:17:45,568 --> 00:17:53,125
And the idea that a gun leaves its own fingerprint on every cartridge passed from the laboratory into everyday language.

201
00:17:53,575 --> 00:18:05,927
Some writers have linked the delay in reporting the murder, and that call box, to the arrival of the nine nine nine emergency number, first introduced in London in 1937.

202
00:18:05,927 --> 00:18:08,338
The direct link is hard to prove.

203
00:18:08,338 --> 00:18:14,917
What the case certainly showed was how alone a country constable was on his beat at night.

204
00:18:15,366 --> 00:18:19,829
George Gutteridge was buried at Warley, not far from where he died.

205
00:18:19,829 --> 00:18:27,422
A memorial stands by the road near Stapleford Abbotts, and the lane where he stopped the car now carries his name.

206
00:18:27,422 --> 00:18:34,149
The revolver is kept in the Metropolitan Police's Crime Museum, on loan from the Essex Police Museum.

207
00:18:34,598 --> 00:18:35,947
He was thirty-six.

208
00:18:35,947 --> 00:18:43,742
He was doing an ordinary part of his job, on an ordinary night, writing down a car number by torchlight.

209
00:18:43,742 --> 00:18:54,384
He is remembered, with respect, by the force he served, and by his family, who lost a husband and a father on an Essex lane in September 1927.

210
00:18:54,833 --> 00:18:56,858
This case file is closed.

211
00:18:56,858 --> 00:19:01,071
